\title{A Combinatorial Bridge Between Balanced Clique Matrices\\ and Clique--Helly Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a conjectured three-way equivalence for finite simple graphs relating a
linear-algebraic property of the clique matrix (\emph{balancedness}), a
graph-theoretic Helly property of maximal cliques (the \emph{hereditary
clique--Helly} property), and a single forbidden induced subgraph (the
octahedron $K_{2,2,2}$, equivalently the complement of a perfect matching
$3K_2$). Our central contribution is the isolation of a single combinatorial
configuration --- a \emph{bad triple} of maximal cliques --- that simultaneously
obstructs \emph{both} balancedness and the clique--Helly property. We prove that
any graph carrying a bad triple is neither balanced nor clique--Helly, that the
octahedron carries such a triple, and that clique--Helly-ness is invariant under
isomorphism. Combining these facts yields two of the three implications of the
conjecture in full generality: every hereditary clique--Helly graph, and every
hereditary balanced graph, is octahedron-free. We discuss the obstacles to the
remaining implications and outline the theory of balanced matrices needed to
complete the program.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} balanced matrix, clique matrix, clique--Helly graph,
hereditary property, octahedron, $K_{2,2,2}$, forbidden induced subgraph, Helly
property.

\section{Introduction}

Two well-studied notions of ``good structure'' in combinatorics arise in very
different settings.

In polyhedral combinatorics and integer programming, a $0/1$ matrix is
\emph{balanced} if it contains no odd square submatrix in which every row and
every column has exactly two ones. Balanced matrices generalize totally balanced
and network matrices, and the linear systems they define enjoy strong
integrality properties.

In structural graph theory, a graph is \emph{clique--Helly} when its maximal
cliques satisfy the Helly property: any pairwise-intersecting subfamily has a
common vertex. The \emph{hereditary} version --- every induced subgraph is
clique--Helly --- is the stable notion and admits a finite forbidden-configuration
characterization.

The clique matrix of a graph --- rows indexed by maximal cliques, columns by
vertices, with a $1$ marking membership --- links the two worlds.

\begin{conjecture}
For every finite simple graph $G$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textup{(i)}] $G$ is balanced: its clique matrix contains no odd square
submatrix with exactly two ones in each row and each column;
\item[\textup{(ii)}] $G$ is hereditary clique--Helly;
\item[\textup{(iii)}] $G$ contains no induced copy of the octahedron
$K_{2,2,2}$, equivalently no induced copy of the complement of $3K_2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{conjecture}

This paper establishes the implications $(i)\Rightarrow(iii)$ and
$(ii)\Rightarrow(iii)$ through a unified mechanism, and lays out the structure
required for the converses. Our organizing principle is a \emph{cross-domain
bridge}: rather than proving the two implications separately, we identify a
single combinatorial object whose presence breaks both properties at once.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $G=(V,E)$ is a finite simple graph; $u \sim v$ denotes adjacency. A
set $S \subseteq V$ is a \emph{clique} if its vertices are pairwise adjacent.

\begin{definition}[Maximal clique]
A finite set $K \subseteq V$ is a \emph{maximal clique} of $G$ if $K$ is a clique
and, for every vertex $v \notin K$, the set $K \cup \{v\}$ is not a clique.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Clique--Helly property]
$G$ is \emph{clique--Helly} if for every finite family $\mathcal{F}$ of maximal
cliques that is pairwise intersecting ($K_1 \cap K_2 \neq \varnothing$ for all
$K_1, K_2 \in \mathcal{F}$), one has $\bigcap_{K \in \mathcal{F}} K \neq
\varnothing$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Clique matrix and balancedness]
The \emph{clique matrix} of $G$ is the $0/1$ matrix $M$ with rows the maximal
cliques, columns the vertices, and $M_{K,v}=1$ iff $v \in K$. We call $G$
\emph{balanced} if $M$ contains no odd square submatrix having exactly two $1$'s
in every row and every column. Explicitly, $G$ fails to be balanced iff there
exist an odd $k$, distinct maximal cliques $K_1,\dots,K_k$, and distinct
vertices $v_1,\dots,v_k$ such that each $K_i$ contains exactly two of the $v_j$
and each $v_j$ lies in exactly two of the $K_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bad triple]
A \emph{bad triple} in $G$ consists of three pairwise-distinct maximal cliques
$K_0,K_1,K_2$ and three pairwise-distinct vertices $a,b,c$ with
\[
a \in K_1 \cap K_2 \setminus K_0, \quad
b \in K_0 \cap K_2 \setminus K_1, \quad
c \in K_0 \cap K_1 \setminus K_2,
\]
and $K_0 \cap K_1 \cap K_2 = \varnothing$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hereditary properties]
$G$ is \emph{hereditary clique--Helly} if every graph $H$ admitting an induced
embedding $H \hookrightarrow G$ is clique--Helly; likewise $G$ is
\emph{hereditary balanced} if every such $H$ is balanced. An induced embedding is
a vertex injection preserving both adjacency and non-adjacency.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Octahedron and its matching]
Label $V=\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$ with antipodal pairs $\{0,1\},\{2,3\},\{4,5\}$. The
\emph{octahedron} $\Oct = K_{2,2,2}$ has $i \sim j$ iff $i \neq j$ and
$\lfloor i/2\rfloor \neq \lfloor j/2\rfloor$. The matching $3K_2$ has $i \sim j$
iff $i \neq j$ and $\lfloor i/2\rfloor = \lfloor j/2\rfloor$.
\end{definition}

\section{The bridge}

\begin{theorem}
If $G$ contains a bad triple, then $G$ is not clique--Helly.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $\mathcal{F}=\{K_0,K_1,K_2\}$, a family of maximal cliques. It is pairwise
intersecting: $c \in K_0 \cap K_1$, $b \in K_0 \cap K_2$, $a \in K_1 \cap K_2$.
But $K_0 \cap K_1 \cap K_2 = \varnothing$, so there is no common vertex, in
violation of the clique--Helly property.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
If $G$ contains a bad triple, then $G$ is not balanced.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take rows $K_0,K_1,K_2$ and columns $a,b,c$ ($k=3$ odd). The incidence pattern
gives
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
 & a & b & c \\ \hline
K_0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\
K_1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\
K_2 & 1 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\]
Each row and each column has exactly two $1$'s, and $3$ is odd; this is a
forbidden submatrix.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
If $e : G \cong H$ is a graph isomorphism and $G$ is clique--Helly, then $H$ is
clique--Helly.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pull a pairwise-intersecting family $\mathcal{F}$ of maximal cliques of $H$ back
through $e^{-1}$; isomorphisms preserve maximal cliques and intersections, so the
image is a pairwise-intersecting family of maximal cliques of $G$, which has a
common vertex $v$. Then $e(v)$ is common to every member of $\mathcal{F}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The octahedron carries a bad triple}

\begin{lemma}
In $\Oct$, each transversal triangle $\{0,2,4\},\{1,2,5\},\{1,3,4\}$ is a maximal
clique.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Vertices from distinct pairs are adjacent, so a transversal triple is a clique;
any fourth vertex repeats an antipodal pair and is non-adjacent to its partner,
so the triangle is maximal.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
The triangles $K_0=\{0,2,4\}$, $K_1=\{1,2,5\}$, $K_2=\{1,3,4\}$ with $a=1$,
$b=4$, $c=2$ form a bad triple of $\Oct$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The three cliques are maximal and distinct. We have $1 \in K_1\cap K_2$,
$1 \notin K_0$; $4 \in K_0 \cap K_2$, $4 \notin K_1$; $2 \in K_0\cap K_1$,
$2 \notin K_2$; the witnesses are distinct and no vertex lies in all three.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The octahedron is neither clique--Helly nor balanced.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}
$(3K_2)^{\comp} = K_{2,2,2}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two distinct vertices are non-adjacent in $3K_2$ iff they lie in different pairs;
complementing turns exactly those into edges, the adjacency of $\Oct$.
\end{proof}

\section{The hereditary implications}

\begin{theorem}[$(ii)\Rightarrow(iii)$]
A hereditary clique--Helly graph contains no induced octahedron.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An induced embedding $\Oct \hookrightarrow G$ would make $\Oct$ clique--Helly by
heredity, contradicting the corollary.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$(i)\Rightarrow(iii)$, matrix side]
A hereditary balanced graph contains no induced octahedron.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Symmetric, using that $\Oct$ is not balanced.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The unified bridge]
The complement of $3K_2$ is the octahedron, and it is simultaneously not balanced
and not clique--Helly; both failures are witnessed by the same bad triple.
\end{theorem}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Bad-triple detector.} Search over unordered triples of maximal cliques
and test the bad-triple pattern (pairwise intersections nonempty, total
intersection empty, cyclic witness vertices). Complexity $O(m^3 n)$ in maximal
cliques $m$ and vertices $n$; a positive result certifies both
non-balancedness and non-clique--Helly-ness.

\paragraph{Balancedness sub-check.} Enumerate candidate odd square submatrices of
a $0/1$ matrix and test the two-per-row-and-column condition; the $3\times3$ case
already captures the octahedral obstruction.

\paragraph{Induced-octahedron search.} Test each $6$-subset for isomorphism to
$K_{2,2,2}$ (a $3$-regular graph whose three non-edges form a perfect matching);
$O(n^6)$ naively.

\section{Applications}

Balanced clique matrices give integral polytopes for set-covering and
set-packing problems, so an octahedron test is a fast necessary certificate
against balancedness. The bad-triple detector yields explicit witnesses of
failure of the clique--Helly property, useful in clique-operator dynamics. And a
single forbidden octahedron, where valid, is the most economical possible
obstruction for structural decompositions.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The two proved implications flow from one obstruction; the converses are harder.
The direction $(iii)\Rightarrow(i)$ is subtle because balancedness concerns the
matrix of \emph{maximal} cliques, while a triangle of an induced octahedron need
not stay maximal in a larger host; a faithful route needs the Berge /
Conforti--Cornu\'ejols--Rao theory of balanced matrices with
closure-under-submatrix. The direction $(iii)\Rightarrow(ii)$ must be reconciled
with Prisner's characterization by three ocular configurations and may require an
extra hypothesis such as distance-hereditariness. A general balanced-matrix
library --- with the bipartite even-cycle reformulation --- and a full clique
enumeration of the octahedron are the natural foundations to build next.

\section{Conclusion}

A single configuration, the bad triple, simultaneously breaks balancedness and
the clique--Helly property. The octahedron $K_{2,2,2}=(3K_2)^{\comp}$ carries
such a triple, yielding two clean hereditary implications toward a conjectured
single-forbidden-subgraph characterization of balanced graphs.

\end{document}

